const HARNESS_NOTES = {
  standards: {
    title: "Organisational standards",
    body: "Security, compliance and SDLC requirements \u2014 the rules that apply no matter which team is writing the code.",
    detail: "These are normally transmitted by onboarding, code review and hope, which is why compliance decays with distance from the person who wrote the rule. Read as an input, they become something a harness can enforce identically for every team."
  },
  platform: {
    title: "Platform documentation \u2014 read, not guessed",
    body: "The harness researches the platform's own documentation through the Confluence MCP rather than relying on what a model remembers about the ecosystem.",
    detail: "This is what makes 'only approved tooling' enforceable. The approved list is a living document owned by the platform organisation; the harness reads that document and constrains generation to it. An agent cannot reach for the popular library it saw a million times in training if the org has standardised on something else \u2014 and when the approved list changes, the harness picks up the change rather than needing an update."
  },
  team: {
    title: "Team conventions",
    body: "Repository shape, which layers of testing this kind of service needs, review expectations, naming.",
    detail: "The part that legitimately differs between teams. Keeping it as an explicit input is what stops a shared harness from flattening every team into one house style it does not want."
  },
  meta: {
    title: "The meta-harness",
    body: "Builds harnesses. It reads the standards, the platform documentation and the team's conventions, and produces the governed environment that team's agents work inside.",
    detail: "This is the leverage. Writing one harness by hand is a project; writing twelve is a department nobody funds. Making harness construction cheap is what gets a governed environment to every team instead of the one that complained loudest \u2014 and it means a standard changing is a regeneration, not a training campaign."
  },
  harness: {
    title: "The harness",
    body: "The governed environment for one team on one platform. It carries the approved toolchain, the required testing layers, the hook set, the deployment and cloud patterns, and the artifacts the org expects a change to arrive with.",
    detail: "Compliance stops being something a reviewer has to remember to check. It is the shape of the workspace: the wrong tool is not available, the missing test layer does not pass the border, the commit hook is already installed. A developer moving between teams gets a different harness, not a different set of things to remember."
  },
  agent: {
    title: "Agent and developer, inside it",
    body: "Both work within the harness. Neither has to hold the standards in their head, and neither can quietly step outside them.",
    detail: "The speed comes from what is no longer in the path: waiting on a specialist to answer whether a tool is allowed, discovering at review time that a test layer is missing, hand-writing deployment scaffolding that has a correct form already. The reviewer's attention moves from conformance to design, which is the only part that needed a human."
  },
  regen: {
    title: "When a standard changes, regenerate",
    body: "A new control, a new approved tool, a new deployment pattern \u2014 change the input and rebuild the harnesses rather than asking every team to absorb the change.",
    detail: "This is the property that compounds. In the usual model a policy change costs an announcement, a wiki edit, a quarter of drift and a follow-up audit. Here it costs a regeneration, and the next change any team makes is already compliant with it."
  }
};
